var Zf = {
  WELCOME: `You own one Cat and a small plot of land. Cats do the work in this game: you send them on shifts at your buildings and they bring back materials and experience. Every Cat has a profession, and a Cat working at the building that matches its profession makes more. Your first Cat is a Lumberjack, so its best place is the Lumber Camp.`,
  WELCOME_BACK: `You left part-way through the tutorial. Nothing was lost: the game remembers which steps you finished, and this picks up at the next one.`,
  FIRST_JOB: `A shift is a job with a length. Click a building on your plot, choose which Cat works there and for how long, then press Start. The Cat works on its own while the timer runs, even if you close the game, and when the timer ends you come back and press Claim to collect what it made.`,
  FAST_TRACK: `Fast Track skips most of the waiting on a running shift. It normally costs $PAWS, the game's currency, but your first one is free so you can see what it does. It only shortens the wait: the materials and experience the shift pays are exactly the same.`,
  CLAIM_FIRST_JOB: `When a shift finishes, what it made sits at the building until you press Claim. Claiming puts the materials into your stock (the Resources dropdown at the top) and gives your Cat experience points, which is how Cats level up.`,
  "CLAIM_FIRST_JOB:tracked": `Your shift was shortened, and the materials and experience it pays are the same as if it had run its whole length. The one thing a Fast Track does not speed up is Reward Round points, which count the real time your Cat worked. While the timer runs there is nothing to press: when it ends, the shift waits at the Lumber Camp for you to claim it.`,
  "CLAIM_FIRST_JOB:wait": `Every building ranks your Cats by how much they would make there, so you never have to work out who is best for a job: the top of the list is. While the timer runs there is nothing to press: when it ends, the shift waits at the Lumber Camp for you to claim it.`,
  MEET_THE_PLOT: `Your plot has a few core buildings that make the basic materials: the Garden makes Food, the Quarry makes Ore, the Lumber Camp makes Wood, the Workshop turns Wood and Ore into Components, and the House looks after your Cats. Materials pay for everything you build, upgrade or recruit. Some buildings also earn Reward Round points, which are worth a share of the prize every round.`,
  TOWN_DIRECTORY: `Town is the shared world where every player's Cat walks around. The buildings there are the game's services: the Trainer levels Cats up, the Tavern recruits new ones, the Permit Office hands out businesses, the Marketplace buys and sells Cats. The Directory at the top of the screen lists them; choose one and your Cat walks there.`,
  VISIT_TRAINER: `The Cat Trainer is where a Cat spends its experience points to go up a level. Higher levels make a Cat produce more on every shift. Walking there through the Directory is how you reach every Town building from now on.`,
  VISIT_TAVERN: `The Recruitment Tavern is where new Cats come from. Each recruit costs materials and some $PAWS and rolls a random profession, rarity and stats. You are only looking today; your first recruit comes a little later.`,
  REACH_LEVEL_3: `Levelling up costs experience points, which shifts pay, plus a small amount of materials. Level 3 is the gate for two things: your free Business Permit and your first recruit. Keep your Cat working until it has the experience, then level it up at the Trainer.`,
  "REACH_LEVEL_3:wait": `A shift pays one experience point a minute, so the timer on the strip is your Cat earning its next level. Nothing needs pressing until it ends. Town is open meanwhile, and the strip keeps the clock wherever you go.`,
  CLAIM_PERMIT: `A Business Permit is how you get a new business for your plot. Every permit is a roll: it gives you one of the businesses on its table, rarer ones less often. Your first permit is free and lands in your Storage tab, ready to place.`,
  PLACE_BUSINESS: `A new business waits in the Storage tab of your Property until you put it on your plot. Press Deploy on it, then click a green square on your land. Green means it fits there; red means something is in the way.`,
  MOVE_BUSINESS: `Nothing on your plot is fixed. Any building can be picked up and put down somewhere else for free, as long as it is not in the middle of a shift. The panel for your new business is open: press Move, then click a new green spot.`,
  ROTATE_BUSINESS: `Rotate turns a building a quarter turn. It only changes which way it faces, so it is purely for how your plot looks. Press Rotate in the open panel.`,
  MANAGE_BUSINESS: `A business has three more controls in its panel. Store takes it off the plot into your Storage tab, keeping its level, so you can free up a slot; Deploy in the Storage tab puts it back, free and instant. Upgrade raises what it produces, for a price that is always shown first. Recycle gives businesses up for a fresh permit roll, and cannot be undone. You are only reading about them here; nothing is pressed.`,
  FIRST_BUSINESS: `Your new business works like the Lumber Camp: click it, choose a Cat, choose a shift, press Start. Basic businesses make a material; advanced ones earn Reward Round points and may use materials to do it. Your land decides how many Cats can work at once and how many businesses can be placed.`,
  START_SECOND_CAT: `One Cat can only be in one place. A second Cat doubles what you make. Recruiting costs a few materials and some $PAWS, and your first recruit's $PAWS is covered by a credit every new account gets. The new Cat arrives after a short wait.`,
  VISIT_REWARDS: `The Rewards Vault shows each round's prizes. Businesses earn points in a category; at the end of each Reward Round the players who earned points in a category share that category's pool in proportion to their points.`,
  VISIT_MARKET: `The Marketplace is where players buy and sell Cats to each other for $PAWS. You do not need to buy anything; it is worth knowing it is there, because a Cat you no longer want can be sold rather than released.`,
  VISIT_LEADERBOARD: `The Leaderboard ranks every player by things like Cat level, roster size and weekly production. It is for comparing, nothing more.`,
  VISIT_STAKING: `Staking means locking some $PAWS away for a while. In return, construction and recruitment timers get shorter, and several locks add up to a maximum. It never pays rewards and nothing in the game needs it, so it is entirely optional.`,
  MEET_SECOND_CAT: `Your recruit is on its way. When the timer ends, go to the Tavern and press Meet your recruit to see what you rolled: its profession, rarity, stats and traits.`,
  "MEET_SECOND_CAT:wait": `Recruiting takes a little while, and the timer runs on its own whether you watch it or not. There is nothing to press until it ends, so this is a good moment to see the rest of Town.`,
  MANAGE_CATS: `Every Cat you own is listed at the Trainer, and this is your second Cat's sheet. It shows everything about the Cat and the things you can do with it: make it your Main Cat (the one that walks Town with you), sell it, or release it.`,
  RELEASE_PREVIEW: `Release removes a Cat from your account for good, with nothing in return. It exists to free a roster slot. This step only opens the warning so you know what it says; you will cancel it, and the tutorial never releases a Cat.`,
  CAT_RULES: `Three rules about Cats. Your Starter Cat can never be sold or released. You can own up to fifty Cats. And how many can work at once is a separate limit set by your land, so you can own more Cats than can work.`,
  TWO_CATS_WORKING: `The point of a second Cat is two shifts at once. Click a station, choose one Cat, choose a shift, press Start, then do the same at another station with your other Cat.`,
  GROW_YOUR_LAND: `Your land has a tier. A bigger tier means more space, more Cats working at once and more business slots. Expanding costs materials and $PAWS and takes a while to build; everything it needs is listed on your Property screen, and the button turns gold when you have it all.`,
  FINISH: `That is the whole loop: send Cats to work, claim what they make, level them up, recruit more, roll permits for new businesses, and grow your land. Your share of every reward pool is on the Rewards screen, and the Game Guide has every rule written down for whenever you need it.`,
  context: `Your Cat has the experience it needs. Levelling up is a roll: you pick the success chance, and higher chances cost more $PAWS. A failed attempt keeps the Cat and its experience and makes the next try more likely to succeed (that is Resolve).`,
  "context:level-up:free": `Your Cat has the experience it needs. Levelling up is a roll: you pick the success chance before you confirm, and a higher chance normally costs more $PAWS. Your first levels cost no $PAWS at all, so right now the 100% option is free and the roll cannot fail: press 100%, then Attempt upgrade. Later on you will weigh the price of better odds against the risk of a failed attempt, which keeps the Cat and its experience and makes the next try more likely.`,
  "lesson:TRAINER": `Shifts give Cats experience points. With enough, a Cat can attempt the next level here. Each attempt has a success chance and a price, both shown before you confirm. A failed attempt does not hurt the Cat: it keeps its level and experience, and the next attempt at that level is more likely to succeed.`,
  "lesson:TAVERN": `New Cats are rolled here. Each one gets a random profession, a rarity that sets how good its stats can be, and some traits. Your first few recruits are quick; each one after takes longer, so recruiting is something you do steadily rather than all at once.`,
  "lesson:REWARDS": `Businesses earn points in a category. Each category has a pool every round. When a round ends, everyone who earned points in a category during it gets a share of its pool in proportion to their points. Fast Track speeds up materials, never points: points follow the real time your Cats worked.`,
  "lesson:MARKET": `Players trade Cats here for $PAWS, minus a small fee. A Cat bought from another player needs a little time to bond with you before it will work.`,
  "lesson:LEADERBOARD": `The boards rank players by different measures. Pick one from the dropdown.`,
  "lesson:STAKING": `Locking $PAWS here shortens construction and recruitment timers while it is locked; several locks add up, to a maximum. It pays nothing else and nothing needs it.`,
};
